  "headline": "12 of 3,308 combinations survived. The best was RSI on INVH at 9.2.",
  "summary": "3,308 indicator-and-stock combinations were tested this week. Each was scored from 1 to 10 on whether it still made money on data it had never seen, after paying realistic trading costs. 12 scored 8 or above, meaning they survived convincingly. 1,297 of 3,308 finished with a positive Sharpe ratio after costs, though a positive Sharpe is a low bar and says nothing about whether the rule beat simply owning the stock. The median score was 2.4. A low median is the expected result rather than a broken test: most published indicator backtests do not subtract trading costs or check performance on unseen data, and doing both is what removes the apparent edge. Treat the 12 survivors with suspicion rather than excitement. Testing 3,308 combinations and reporting the best of them is precisely how a search produces results that do not repeat \u2014 at 0.4% of everything tested, this is close to what pure chance would throw up. A combination is only interesting if it survives again next week, on data that did not exist when it was selected.",
